1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,500
Four minutes. Six steps. One litre of used cooking oil.

2
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:14,400
In the Pavuna–Meriti basin, thousands of small kitchens fry food every day.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:20,000
When the oil is used up, there is no one to sell it to — so it goes down the drain.

4
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:25,800
A single litre can contaminate up to 25,000 litres of water.

5
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:32,400
Inside the pipes it cools, hardens and traps everything else: a fatberg.

6
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:38,300
A blocked drain has nowhere to send the rain.

7
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:43,400
About 13,000 unblocking operations a year in the Baixada Fluminense.

8
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,700
The water rises in the same low-income streets, every season.

9
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:52,900
Here is the strange part: that oil is worth money.

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,200
The catadores who already work these streets live on cardboard, at R$0.15–0.40 a kilo.

11
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:06,600
The oil going past them sells for R$0.80–2.50 a litre — and demand for it grew over 700% in a decade.

12
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,200
The workers exist. The buyers exist.

13
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:19,800
What is missing is the layer in between — something that collects the oil and proves what it is.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,400
That layer is the collector system. It runs in six steps.

15
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:31,800
Step one. A small restaurant, bar or snack shop signs up at the residents' association.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,800
It gets a clean PET bottle with a code of its own — and picks a fixed collection day.

17
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:43,200
Nothing to buy, nothing to pay. The bottle is the account.

18
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:49,200
Households on the same street can join too, and earn Green Points at local shops.

19
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,400
Step two. A catador from the local cooperative walks the route.

20
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:01,400
At the door they check the oil through the clear bottle, and scan the code.

21
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:08,200
That one scan records four things: how much, from where, when, and who collected it.

22
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:14,000
The oil is no longer anonymous waste. It has a history.

23
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,600
The shop keeps a receipt for it.

24
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,800
Step three. Everything goes to a neighbourhood hub, hosted by the residents' association.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,800
Small pickups are pooled into a single batch, and the oil is tested for water and dirt.

26
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:36,400
The batch can only ship if the total matches the sum of every scan.

27
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:42,000
That is what stops oil from quietly disappearing on the way.

28
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:45,600
Twenty-four small deliveries, one contract-grade batch.

29
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:52,400
Step four. The batch is sold under contract, to several processors — never just one.

30
00:02:52,800 --> 00:03:00,000
Rio learned this the hard way: the old PROVE programme depended on a single refinery.

31
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:05,800
When it was shut down in 2026, the collected oil had nowhere to go.

32
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:09,600
More than one buyer keeps the system alive.

33
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:15,600
Step five. The money comes back to the same streets, by a rule fixed in advance.

34
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:20,600
Forty percent goes to the catadores who collected it.

35
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:27,000
Twenty percent to households that separate their oil, twenty percent as Green Points spent in local shops,

36
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:34,600
and twenty percent to the Faetequinho course, which already trades free classes for used oil in Jardim América.

37
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,600
In the pilot that is R$113,517 a year, going back where the oil came from.

38
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:45,000
Step six. All of those scans are data, and the data feeds back.

39
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:50,600
The system forecasts how much oil each zone will produce next month,

40
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:56,400
redraws the routes so collectors walk less and carry more,

41
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:02,400
and flags the shops that are about to drop out, before they do.

42
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:09,200
Two dashboards share what it sees: one for the city, one for buyers looking for verified supply.

43
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:13,700
So, the whole cycle:

44
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,400
Kitchen. Scan. Hub. Contract. Payment. And data that makes the next round cheaper.

45
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,600
The same litre that used to harden inside a pipe now pays a wage.

46
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,400
In the Jardim América pilot: 75,678 litres recovered a year.

47
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:42,200
R$113,517 of revenue against R$105,000 of cost — the pilot pays for itself.

48
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:50,200
R$1.27 of social value for every R$1 invested, and around 91 tonnes of CO₂ avoided.

49
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,700
Same oil. A different path.

50
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:00,800
EcoCycle — a collection system for the last mile.
